  "learn": [
    {
      "id": "learn",
      "title": "What does a percentage mean?",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Per cent means out of 100. A percentage tells us how many equal parts out of a hundred we have.",
        "100% is the whole amount, 50% is half, and 0% is none. The whole amount can be any size."
      ],
      "equation": "37% = 37/100 = 0.37"
    },
    {
      "id": "same-amount",
      "title": "The same amount, three ways",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Fractions, decimals and percentages can describe the same share. Divide a percentage by 100 to write it as a decimal.",
        "To write a percentage as a fraction, put it over 100 and simplify. To turn a decimal into a percentage, multiply by 100."
      ],
      "equation": "50% = 1/2 = 0.5   ·   20% = 1/5 = 0.2",
      "note": "5% = 0.05, not 0.5. One fifth is 20%, not 5%."
    },
    {
      "id": "colour-a-percentage",
      "title": "Colour three quarters",
      "paragraphs": [
        "This circle has 20 equal sectors. Each sector is 5% of the whole. Tap a sector to colour it; tap it again to clear it.",
        "Colour 75% of the circle, then check your answer."
      ],
      "equation": "75% = 75/100 = 3/4 = 0.75",
      "colour": 75
    },
    {
      "id": "use-a-fraction",
      "title": "Use a familiar fraction",
      "paragraphs": [
        "To find a fraction of an amount, divide by the denominator, then multiply by the numerator. For 75% = 3/4, divide the whole amount by 4, then multiply by 3.",
        "A box contains 32 pencils. Three quarters of them are blue. How many are blue?"
      ],
      "equation": "75% of 32 = 32 ÷ 4 × 3 = 24",
      "note": "Compare the actual amounts, not just the percentages. Here, 75% of 32 is 24. But 60% of 50 is 30 (10% is 5, so 60% is 6 × 5). The smaller percentage gives the larger amount because the wholes are different."
    },
    {
      "id": "building-blocks",
      "title": "Build from 10% and 1%",
      "paragraphs": [
        "To find 10%, divide by 10. To find 1%, divide by 100. For 5%, halve the 10% amount.",
        "Find 35% of £80: 10% is £8, so 30% is £24. Another 5% is £4. Add the parts."
      ],
      "equation": "35% of £80 = £24 + £4 = £28",
      "note": "Choose the simplest pieces for the numbers. For 26% of 180, use 25% + 1%: 180 ÷ 4 = 45 and 180 ÷ 100 = 1.8. Add them to get 46.8. You do not always need to start with 10%."
    },
    {
      "id": "one-percent",
      "title": "Small parts are useful",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Find 12% of 350 g. First find 10%: 350 ÷ 10 = 35 g. Then find 1%: 350 ÷ 100 = 3.5 g.",
        "12% is 10% + 2%, so add 35 g and twice 3.5 g."
      ],
      "equation": "12% of 350 g = 35 g + 7 g = 42 g",
      "note": "For a comparison question, calculate each percentage of its own whole, then compare the results. Show both calculations and finish with a sentence saying which amount is greater."
    },
    {
      "id": "multipliers",
      "title": "Of means multiply",
      "paragraphs": [
        "For any percentage, divide it by 100 and multiply by the whole amount. A calculator makes this useful for awkward percentages.",
        "For 23% of 450, the multiplier is 0.23."
      ],
      "equation": "23% of 450 = 0.23 × 450 = 103.5",
      "note": "Finding 25% of an amount uses 0.25. Increasing or decreasing an amount is a different question, covered in a later lesson."
    },
    {
      "id": "over-100",
      "title": "More than the whole",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Percentages can be greater than 100%. 150% is one whole and another half.",
        "For positive amounts, less than 100% gives a smaller amount; more than 100% gives a larger amount."
      ],
      "equation": "150% of 46 = 1.5 × 46 = 69",
      "note": "Always identify the whole amount before calculating a percentage."
    }
  ],
